-      "title": "Canon is never retro-corrected",
-      "description": "The facts are immutable; the telling is repairable — the settled principle from Alan's principle-level pass. Committed events, choices, outcomes, and revealed truths are never rewritten to fit new information (it lands FORWARD as new canon); repairing defective CRAFT in a committed chapter — POV drift, register slips, voice continuity — is legitimate only as an explicit, visible act that preserves every fact (live precedent: aria's sanctioned ch16/ch17 in-place prose repair), never a silent edit. A retroactive change to committed FACTS is legitimate only when Alan specifically requests or approves it, as correction of a real mistake. Surfaced in the umbrella evidence (#14527); reinforced by the dragons reconcile-on-wake rule that committed rows WIN over a draft or an implied state."
