+      "title": "Spoken register — dialogue sounds like people talking; transparent speech, neither composed nor costumed",
+      "description": "Alan's ruling, two calibration passes (2026-07-10/11). Pass 1: dialogue read 'very literary' — composed periodic essays in mouths, speech-as-thesis (exhibit: Long Glass T12) — and should be casual/documentary, scoped to BOTH dialogue and POV interiority. Pass 2, reading run 5's opening turns live: the v17 'Huckleberry Finn' calibration OVERSHOT into the discordant pole — heavy vernacular texture (dropped subjects, folksy idiom, performed casualness) is itself a costume that calls attention to the diction; Alan re-centered the target to the middle ('Brandon Sanderson maybe') — clean, natural, contemporary, transparent. The register spectrum now carries TWO named failure poles (THE COMPOSED LINE / THE PERFORMED VERNACULAR) with transparency as the principle between them: window-pane extended to speech and thought — the reader hears the PERSON, never the diction. Evidence base unchanged from v17: the blind voice-attribution census (one closing intimacy dialect, shared lyric interiority engine, 'let'-verb rate rising G1→G4) plus the run-4 A/B proving band-naming displaces telling pressure — this policy remains a SUBSTRATE change (what authors write from), with voice distinctness carried by attention/agenda/attitude/economy rather than dialect texture."
